        "can_see_hidden_post": false,
        "can_wiki": false,
        "read": false,
        "user_title": null,
        "bookmarked": false,
        "raw": "<p>In a recent game I was playing, the players finally got a change to use their Misdirection abilities. And realized that unlike any other ability, Misdirection punishes the characters for rolling high. This is the only power we've found where you get a better result (more damage passed on to a minion) if you roll low.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The description of the ability is:&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>When a nearby hero in the Yellow or Red zone would take damage, Defend against that damage by rolling your single [power] die, then redirect any remaining damage to a nearby minion of your choice.</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>So the better you roll on the defense, the less remaining damage there is to redirect to a minion. While it's still a useful power (it saved a lot of damage being done to a key character), they players felt that they were missing something since their good defend rolls resulted in no damage to redirect.&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Any comments? Are we missing something? Anyone have a sugested house rule to emphasies the damage redirection?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>One thought I had is to allow them to change the power description to this:</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>When a nearby hero in the Yellow or Red zone would take damage, roll&nbsp;your single [power] die to either boost or hinder the damage, then redirect any remaining damage to a nearby minion of your choice if any are available.</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>This allows all the damage to be redirected + more for a good roll. Downside is that it's much less effective if there are no minions around or if the minions are freindly. Comments?</p>",
